#: src/macros/proc-macros/writing.md:6
msgid "Writing Procedural Macros"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/writing.md:8
msgid ""
"Writing procedural macros is basically about learning how to work with the "
"standard helper libraries designed specifically to parse and generate Rust "
"code."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/writing.md:11
msgid ""
"Because dealing with raw, low-level `TokenStream`s manually is tedious and "
"difficult, we use a standard suite of specialized helper libraries:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/writing.md:14
msgid "**`proc_macro`:** Provided by the compiler to interface token streams."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/writing.md:15
msgid ""
"**`proc_macro2`:** Wraps `proc_macro` to allow unit testing and out-of-"
"compiler executions."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/writing.md:17
msgid ""
"**`syn`:** A complete parser library that parses Rust token streams into "
"structured Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) structures."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/writing.md:19
msgid ""
"**`quote`:** A templated code generation library that turns your Rust "
"structures back into token streams."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/writing.md:22
msgid "We will look at how each of these crates builds on top of one another."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/writing.md:27
msgid ""
"Consider how writing a proc macro is essentially writing a mini-compiler: "
"you parse a string of tokens (lexical analysis), match its structure "
"(parsing), and generate a new string of tokens (transformation and code "
"emission)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/proc-macros/writing.md:30
msgid ""
"Reassure students that you rarely ever work with raw tokens directly. The "
"`syn` and `quote` crates do 95% of the heavy parsing and formatting work for "
"you, allowing you to write more-readable macro code."
msgstr ""
